271	16	269	Percussion & Rhythm Instruments	Percussion and rhythm instruments are the heartbeat of music, especially for young learners. These instruments produce sound when they are struck, shaken, scraped, or beaten with hands, sticks, or beaters. In Class 1 music, children are introduced to simple and engaging instruments like the drums, tambourine, maracas, triangle, and cymbals. Because they focus heavily on keeping a steady beat rather than playing different musical notes, these instruments are perfect for helping children understand the foundational elements of time, pace, and energy in music. They encourage active physical participation, making music both tactile and fun.	chapter	8	4	Rhythmic Awareness: Children learn to identify, mimic, and maintain a steady pulse or beat, which is the foundation of all musical training.\n\nMotor Skills Development: Striking a drum or shaking maracas helps develop fine and gross motor skills, as well as hand-eye coordination.\n\nAuditory Discrimination: Students learn to distinguish between different textures of sound, such as the sharp "ting" of a triangle versus the deep "boom" of a drum.\n\nSocial Collaboration: Playing rhythm instruments in a group teaches children how to listen to others, wait for their turn, and play in unison, building essential teamwork and social skills.	{}	t	\N	\N	2026-06-11 18:09:15.406768	2026-06-11 18:09:15.406768
