412	22	\N	Hamara Khaan-Paan (हमारा खान-पान - Our Food and Drinks)	The unit "Hamara Khaan-Paan" (हमारा खान-पान) introduces young learners to the essential world of food, drinks, and dietary habits through engaging stories, interactive pictures, and playful poems. It emphasizes the diversity of what we eat, tracking how food travels from fields to our plates, and explores traditional Indian foods like grains, vegetables, and puffed-up rotis. By focusing on everyday items like potatoes, corn, and traditional meals, the unit connects language learning with the students' real-world sensory experiences, fostering an early curiosity about nutrition and food sources.	unit	3	4	Upon completing this unit, students successfully develop foundational vocabulary related to various food items, tastes, and culinary processes while enhancing their reading, speaking, and listening skills in Hindi. They gain a clear, age-appropriate understanding of healthy eating habits, the importance of avoiding food wastage, and a deep appreciation for the hard work of farmers who grow our food. Ultimately, the unit instills mindful eating practices and sparks positive family conversations around daily nutrition, ensuring that language acquisition goes hand-in-hand with vital life skills.	{}	t	\N	\N	2026-06-13 16:18:07.560897	2026-06-13 16:18:07.560897
391	21	384	Opposites & Synonyms	The Opposites & Synonyms chapter is designed as a vibrant, interactive introduction to vocabulary building for Class 2 students. At this foundational stage, the chapter moves away from rote memorization and focuses on helping children understand how words relate to one another in everyday speech. Through engaging visual aids, matching games, and simple storytelling, students explore Synonyms (words that mean the same, like happy and glad) and Opposites/Antonyms (words that mean the reverse, like big and small). The lesson emphasizes spoken practice, encouraging children to use these new words in full sentences to express their feelings, describe their surroundings, and compare objects. By linking word pairs to familiar concepts—like the weather, emotions, and sizes—the chapter makes expanding vocabulary an intuitive and joyful experience.	chapter	17	4	Identify and Differentiate: Clearly distinguish between words that have similar meanings (synonyms) and words that have contrasting meanings (opposites).\n\nExpand Spoken Vocabulary: Express themselves more fluidly by replacing overused words with appropriate synonyms (e.g., saying "The cake is delicious" instead of just "good").\n\nEnhance Descriptive Skills: Use exact opposite pairs to describe changes in environment, emotions, and physical attributes (e.g., "The tea is hot, but the ice cream is cold").\n\nImprove Sentence Construction: Confidently build and speak complete, grammatically correct sentences using newly learned word pairs during class discussions and role-plays.\n\nDevelop Listening Comprehension: Recognize context clues in spoken English to grasp the meaning of an unfamiliar word based on its synonym or opposite.	{}	t	\N	\N	2026-06-13 13:01:06.002254	2026-06-13 13:01:31.881478
392	21	\N	Writing & Composition Skills	The Writing and Composition section for Class 2 Spoken English serves as a bridge between foundational phonics and expressive communication. At this developmental stage, the curriculum focuses on transitioning young learners from writing isolated words to structuring simple, coherent sentences. Through highly interactive and visual exercises—such as picture composition, guided sentence completion, and short creative storytelling—students learn to express their thoughts, daily routines, and emotions on paper. The curriculum places a strong emphasis on correct punctuation (capital letters, full stops, and question marks) and proper word order. By connecting spoken vocabulary with written expressions, this section nurtures imagination, builds core grammatical confidence, and encourages students to communicate their ideas clearly and independently.	section	6	4	Construct Basic Sentences: Formulate grammatically correct simple sentences independently using appropriate subject-verb agreement and descriptive words (adjectives).\n\nInterpret Visual Cues: Analyze images and describe scenes, actions, or objects effectively in writing through structured picture composition.\n\nApply Foundational Punctuation: Demonstrate the correct use of capital letters at the beginning of sentences and proper nouns, alongside full stops and question marks at the end.\n\nExpress Personal Experiences: Write short, coherent paragraphs (3–5 sentences) about familiar topics, such as "My Family," "My School," or "My Favorite Toy."\n\nSequence Ideas Logically: Organize thoughts in a clear chronological order when recounting a short story or a daily routine.\n\nEnhance Vocabulary Integration: Actively use newly acquired spoken English vocabulary and sight words appropriately within their written exercises.	{}	t	\N	\N	2026-06-13 13:08:54.373643	2026-06-13 13:08:54.373643
393	21	392	Picture Composition	The "Picture Composition" chapter is designed as an interactive and visual gateway to help young learners transition from spoken observations to structured written expression. At the Class 2 level, children are naturally drawn to vibrant visuals. This chapter utilizes colorful, relatable scenes—such as a bustling park, a birthday party, a rainy day, or a classroom—to spark their imagination. Students are guided through a step-by-step process: first, they look closely at the image; second, they identify key elements (nouns, actions, and colors) using guided prompts and word banks; and finally, they compose short, simple sentences. By linking visual cues with vocabulary, the chapter gently introduces basic grammar concepts like prepositions (in, on, under) and action verbs, making writing feel less like a chore and more like a creative puzzle.	chapter	18	4	Enhanced Observational Skills: Students will learn to pay attention to details, identifying characters, objects, and background elements within a visual scene.\n\nVocabulary Expansion: Through targeted word banks, children will acquire and correctly use new nouns, descriptive adjectives, and action verbs.\n\nSentence Construction: Learners will progress from uttering single-word answers to framing 4 to 5 grammatically correct, simple sentences independently.\n\nCognitive & Logical Thinking: Students will develop the ability to connect visual actions with cause and effect, allowing them to sequence thoughts logically (e.g., "The boy has an umbrella because it is raining").\n\nBoosted Confidence in Speaking and Writing: By successfully describing a picture aloud before writing it down, children will bridge the gap between spoken fluency and written composition, building early confidence in their language skills.	{}	t	\N	\N	2026-06-13 13:10:49.413933	2026-06-13 13:10:49.413933
394	21	392	Sentence Formation	In this chapter, young learners transition from speaking in isolated words to constructing complete, meaningful thoughts. Designed specifically for Class 2, the module focuses on the fundamental building blocks of speech and writing in an interactive, age-appropriate manner. Students are introduced to the concept that a sentence must start with a capital letter, end with a punctuation mark (like a period or question mark), and contain a doing word (verb) to make sense. Through engaging spoken drills, picture-matching activities, and "jumbled word" games, children learn how correct word order changes a random string of words into a clear message. The chapter emphasizes oral confidence, encouraging students to express their daily needs, feelings, and descriptions of their surroundings in full sentences rather than single-word answers.	chapter	19	4	Identify and Construct Basic Sentences: Understand the basic structure of a sentence and independently arrange jumbled words into grammatically correct, complete thoughts.\n\nApply Essential Punctuation: Demonstrate the habitual use of capital letters at the beginning of sentences and appropriate terminal punctuation (full stops and question marks) when speaking and writing.\n\nExpress Ideas Confidently: Shift from using fragmented, one-word responses to speaking in full sentences during daily classroom interactions, show-and-tell activities, and peer conversations.\n\nRecognize Subject-Verb Agreement: Naturally apply simple subject-verb combinations (e.g., "The dog barks" vs. "The dogs bark") in their spoken English without relying on heavy technical jargon.\n\nEnhance Descriptive Skills: Use simple adjectives and verbs to form descriptive sentences about familiar objects, animals, and people in their immediate environment.	{}	t	\N	\N	2026-06-13 13:12:36.123665	2026-06-13 13:12:36.123665
