403	22	400	Khilonewala (खिलौनेवाला) - Story	The story/poem revolves around the immense joy and excitement a child feels when a toy-seller (Khilonewala) enters the neighborhood with a basket full of vibrant toys like cars, trains, bows, and dolls. While other children enthusiastically buy toys to play with, the main character reflects a deep emotional maturity rooted in family values. Instead of asking for casual toys, he asks his mother (Amma) for a bow and arrow or a sword, imagining himself as Lord Rama. He states that he will protect his family, destroy evil forces, and gladly go to exile if his mother asks, though he sweetly worries how he would survive in the forest without her loving embrace.	chapter	3	4	The central outcome of this chapter is teaching children the sanctity of family bonds, selfless love, and moral responsibility. It helps young minds look beyond materialistic desires (like temporary toys) and understand the value of parental devotion, respect, and emotional security found within a household. Additionally, it encourages children to cultivate courage, differentiate between right and wrong, and appreciate the protective, nurturing role a family plays in their lives.	{}	t	\N	\N	2026-06-13 13:57:42.438671	2026-06-13 13:57:42.438671
