1050	38	1048	Odd One Out	In Class 3 General Knowledge, the Odd One Out topic introduces children to basic logical reasoning and mental ability. In this chapter, students are presented with a group of words, objects, or pictures that share a common characteristic, along with one item that does not fit the pattern. The core task is to analyze the attributes of each item—such as shape, size, color, category, or function—and identify the one that is different from the rest. For instance, in a group containing an apple, a banana, an orange, and a potato, students must recognize that the potato is the "odd one out" because it is a vegetable while the others are fruits. This topic serves as a foundational step in building critical thinking, encouraging kids to look beyond surface-level details and understand relationships between different concepts.	chapter	33	4	The primary learning outcome of this chapter is the enhancement of a child's analytical and categorization skills. By practicing these exercises, students learn how to observe details sharply, sort information systematically, and justify their choices based on logic. It strengthens their cognitive abilities, improves vocabulary, and expands general knowledge as they classify things from daily life, such as animals, transport, plants, and shapes. Ultimately, this builds problem-solving confidence, training young minds to identify patterns and anomalies quickly, which is a vital skill for both higher-level mathematics and everyday decision-making.	{}	t	\N	\N	2026-06-20 05:53:15.963963	2026-06-20 05:53:15.963963
1046	38	1043	First Aid Basics and Emergency Contact Numbers	The chapter First Aid Basics and Emergency Contact Numbers introduces young learners to the essential, life-saving concepts of immediate medical assistance and safety awareness. It explains first aid as the very first help given to an injured or sick person before a doctor or ambulance arrives. Through simple, relatable examples, students learn how to handle minor everyday mishaps, such as cleaning a small scrape with antiseptic, applying an ice pack to a bump, or treating a mild burn with cool running water. Additionally, the chapter teaches children how to stay calm during an emergency and features a critical guide to memorizing vital helpline numbers, ensuring they know exactly how and when to call for adult or professional assistance.	chapter	30	4	By completing this unit, students will develop vital personal safety habits and the ability to respond responsibly during unexpected situations. They will be able to identify the basic items found inside a first aid box and understand their proper uses. Beyond practical knowledge, the lesson fosters critical life skills like empathy, quick thinking, and emotional resilience, empowering children to help others in distress without panicking. Most importantly, students will memorize key emergency contact numbers and understand the correct protocol for reporting an emergency, instilling a strong sense of civic duty and community safety from an early age.	{}	t	\N	\N	2026-06-20 05:45:00.476563	2026-06-20 05:45:00.476563
1048	38	\N	Mixed Bag (Logical Reasoning & Mental Ability)	The "Mixed Bag" unit is a dynamic collection of puzzles, patterns, and brain teasers designed to step outside traditional memorization and activate a child's critical thinking skills. At the Class 3 level, this unit introduces students to age-appropriate challenges such as completing number and shape sequences, decoding simple secret messages, identifying odd-one-out items, and solving basic spatial riddles. It presents learning as a game, using colorful visuals and engaging problems to challenge young minds to observe, analyze, and think steps ahead.	unit	8	4	By completing this unit, students will develop essential cognitive and problem-solving skills, learning how to approach unfamiliar problems with confidence and patience. They will be able to recognize underlying visual and numerical patterns, categorize information logically, and use deductive reasoning to find solutions. Ultimately, this builds a strong foundation for mental math, spatial awareness, and independent decision-making, transforming them into more curious and logical thinkers in both academics and everyday life.	{}	t	\N	\N	2026-06-20 05:50:44.085036	2026-06-20 05:50:44.085036
1052	38	1048	Important Calendar Days	The chapter on Important Calendar Days introduces young learners to the significant national and international days celebrated throughout the year, such as Independence Day, Earth Day, and Teacher's Day. It explains why specific dates are marked on our calendars, teaching the historical events, cultural traditions, or global causes behind them. Through fun quizzes, matching exercises, and date-finding puzzles, the lessons connect abstract dates to real-world celebrations, making history and civic awareness highly relatable for eight-year-olds.	chapter	35	4	By studying this chapter, students develop a strong sense of time, chronology, and cultural awareness, learning to appreciate both national pride and global citizenship. It sharpens their logical reasoning and mental ability as they learn to calculate time gaps between events, identify patterns in the calendar, and associate specific themes with correct dates. Ultimately, the chapter helps children understand the value of remembrance and gratitude, transforming a standard calendar from a mere grid of numbers into a meaningful map of human achievements and shared global values.	{}	t	\N	\N	2026-06-20 05:55:56.275416	2026-06-20 05:55:56.275416
